const GNW_EMU_SM511_HTML = "<section class=\"gbemu-hero\"><div class=\"gbemu-hero-num\"><b>SM511 / SM512</b><span>clean-room core</span></div><div class=\"gbemu-hero-txt\"><h3>How to build this chip, exactly as our Game &amp; Watch core runs it</h3><p>This tab is the complete build specification for the <b>SM511 / SM512</b>: every register, every opcode with its timing and skips, reset, halt and wake, the divider, the display, the input, the sound, and every question no datasheet decides, answered the way this core answers it. The shared sections below it carry the implementation contract, the per-title data, save state, the corpus recipe and the acceptance tests a build must pass. Every claim carries its tier.</p></div></section>\n<nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>On this tab</b> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512\">Sharp SM511 and SM512, with the melody engine: build specification</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-readme\">The Game &amp; Watch build specification: index, reading order, rules</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-00-machine\">The machine, whole: orientation before the part chapters</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-60-abi\">The implementation contract (ABI)</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-50-save-state\">The machine's save state: what must survive, and how the reference carries it</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-40-titles\">Per-title data: the 61 titles, their images, artwork, wiring, clock and demo</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-70-acceptance\">The acceptance tests a build must pass</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-80-corpus\">The corpus: how to obtain the images, and how to know you have the right ones</a></nav>\n<section class=\"sect gwemu-chapter\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512\">Sharp SM511 and SM512, with the melody engine: build specification</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s1\">1. Scope</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s2\">2. Registers and memory</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s3\">3. Program counter and program memory layout</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s4\">4. Instruction set</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s5\">5. Execution model</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s6\">6. Reset (ACL) state</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s7\">7. Standby, halt and wake</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s8\">8. Divider and the one-second flag</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s9\">9. Display</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s10\">10. Input</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s11\">11. Sound</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s12\">12. Registered choices</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s13\">13. Constants</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s14\">14. Self-check vectors</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s15\">15. Where this chapter's facts came from</a></nav>\n<p><em>Written 2026-10-03. Supersedes every earlier description of this part.</em></p>\n<p>This chapter specifies the SM511/SM512 machine as this core implements it and as the site ABI presents it. It covers 19 program images (4,096-byte files, 4,032 usable words), 19 melody memories (256 six-bit words each) and the 27 site titles that run them: 23 as SM511 and 4 as SM512. The SM511 is a 4-bit Sharp CPU. It has a 64-page × 63-step program memory addressed through a polynomial P_L counter, 128 nibbles of RAM (two display files, R and S), a two-level return stack, a 15-stage divider that runs off a 32.768 kHz crystal, a <b>system clock the program selects</b> (16.384 or 8.192 kHz, so one instruction slot is 2 or 4 crystal ticks), a W′→W strobe pair, and a crystal-clocked <b>melody engine</b> that drives the R pin. The SM512 is the same machine with a third display file, Q. The instruction table is [90] p170. Three first-byte encodings and two prefix second bytes are not printed there (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT sm511|sm-511|sm512|sm-512 ; instruction set|instruction|opcode.). This core takes them from another emulator's source (SOURCE-READ), and §4 marks each of them. Where no page decides a behaviour, this chapter gives the core's own answer and a tier label. A builder who follows the text exactly gets this core's ABI trajectory bit for bit.</p>\n<p>Notation. <em>Tick</em> means one 32.768 kHz crystal period. <em>Slot</em> means one instruction cycle, which is <code>clkDiv</code> ticks long. <em>Step</em> means one P_L position. \"PC+1\" always means the next polynomial step (§3), never arithmetic +1. Hex values are written <code>0xNN</code>. Bit i of a register is written <code>X_i</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s1\">1. Scope</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parts</td><td>SM511, SM512 (one class, two profiles)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p169 (one table, one datasheet)</td></tr><tr><td>Program images</td><td>19, each 4,096 bytes, indexed <code>(P_U&lt;&lt;10)|(P_M&lt;&lt;6)|P_L</code></td><td>MECHANISM (64 pages × 64 slots; P_L = 0x3F slots are never reached by stepping, §3)</td></tr><tr><td>Melody memories</td><td>19, each 256 bytes, one 6-bit word per byte (bits 7–6 always 0)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p94 (melody memory, 256 x 6); INSTRUMENT <code>tools/melody_memory_audit.mjs</code>: bits 6–7 never set in 4,864 words</td></tr><tr><td>Site titles</td><td>27 (23 SM511, 4 SM512), listed below</td><td>TABLE-READ (the site's <code>cpuType</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Which part an image is</td><td><b>a caller statement.</b> No byte of an image separates SM512 from SM511</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p169: same program memory capacity and same instruction set in both columns. INSTRUMENT <code>tools/sm512_separation.mjs</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Per-title constants.</b> The part comes from the site's own title table. Reason: the chip is named on the package, not in the program. The image and melody files are the corpus file names. Hashes are SHA-256, first 12 hex digits.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>site title(s)</th><th>part</th><th>program image</th><th>program sha256</th><th>melody</th><th>melody sha256</th><th>frame-identical to port (§11.6)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>smbn</code>, <code>smb</code>, <code>smbspecial</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>ym-801.program</code></td><td><code>d4a1eb9d9401</code></td><td><code>ym-801.melody</code></td><td><code>05a26b5f9e2a</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>climbern</code>, <code>climber</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>dr-802.program</code></td><td><code>aa151f4a31d8</code></td><td><code>dr-802.melody</code></td><td><code>98cace3b07ed</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>bfightn</code>, <code>bfight</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>bf-803.program</code></td><td><code>38bfbb54eedb</code></td><td><code>bf-803.melody</code></td><td><code>7f17e2032b8a</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>boxing</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>bx-301_744.program</code></td><td><code>927b582f26ee</code></td><td><code>bx-301_744.melody</code></td><td><code>6e4736ebada0</code></td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>dkong3</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>ak-302.program</code></td><td><code>00db2b7d6060</code></td><td><code>ak-302.melody</code></td><td><code>b2791b378620</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>dkhockey</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>hk-303.program</code></td><td><code>7b358188e341</code></td><td><code>hk-303.melody</code></td><td><code>1d7d2dfbbf3a</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>mariotj</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>mb-108.program</code></td><td><code>257aed0bb880</code></td><td><code>mb-108.melody</code></td><td><code>7b0d8a978483</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>pinball</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>pb-59.program</code></td><td><code>9dc1b7e3b7a7</code></td><td><code>pb-59.melody</code></td><td><code>ec11a70e9fa7</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>sbuster</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>jb-63.program</code></td><td><code>254d103f04ea</code></td><td><code>jb-63.melody</code></td><td><code>eac894385cc9</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>dkjrp</code>, <code>dkjrt</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>cj-93.program</code></td><td><code>ccbfa247ea98</code></td><td><code>cj-93.melody</code></td><td><code>c0b8625e7e40</code></td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>mmousep</code>, <code>dkcirc</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>dc-95.program</code></td><td><code>e9fc7e4858c4</code></td><td><code>dc-95.melody</code></td><td><code>cf90bba51dc0</code></td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>mbaway</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>tb-94.program</code></td><td><code>8698ee42695d</code></td><td><code>tb-94.melody</code></td><td><code>2657ae126ec3</code></td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>snoopyp</code>, <code>snoopyt</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>sm-91.program</code></td><td><code>921f7b168359</code></td><td><code>sm-91.melody</code></td><td><code>2c1e9bfc8005</code></td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>popeyet</code>, <code>popeyep</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>pg-92.program</code></td><td><code>fb0621194a49</code></td><td><code>pg-92.melody</code></td><td><code>6c46c57f8573</code></td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>mariocmt</code></td><td>SM511</td><td><code>cm-72.program</code></td><td><code>6f997b63a6ad</code></td><td><code>cm-72.melody</code></td><td><code>6e22de86c901</code></td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><code>bombsweep</code></td><td>SM512</td><td><code>bd-62.program</code></td><td><code>4f559e796947</code></td><td><code>bd-62.melody</code></td><td><code>3c45f3d0c049</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>gcliff</code></td><td>SM512</td><td><code>mv-64.program</code></td><td><code>426a433e046a</code></td><td><code>mv-64.melody</code></td><td><code>242b2b65aa51</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>zelda</code></td><td>SM512</td><td><code>zl-65.program</code></td><td><code>6a53d017f6b1</code></td><td><code>zl-65.melody</code></td><td><code>4dbee2865abc</code></td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>bjack</code></td><td>SM512</td><td><code>bj-60.program</code></td><td><code>3863a70f0181</code></td><td><code>bj-60.melody</code></td><td><code>771d34cd866e</code></td><td>yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>16 of 27</b> titles are frame-identical to the reference port [INSTRUMENT <code>tools/site_parity.mjs</code>]. <code>pinball</code> and <code>sbuster</code> became identical once the site surface held B and BA pulled up (§10). The other 11 differ because the reference port's melody address freezes (§11.6), and there this core is the reference. <em>Superseded (2026-10-03): 14 of 27, with <code>pinball</code> and <code>sbuster</code> marked no.</em></p>\n<p>The ABI surface is the site's CPU interface: <code>step</code>, <code>reset</code>, <code>pc</code>, <code>halt</code>, <code>r</code>, <code>clkDiv</code>, <code>inB</code>, <code>inBA</code>, <code>inRows</code>, <code>inFixedRow</code>, <code>rMuxInvert</code>, <code>rOut</code>, <code>ram</code>, <code>lcd</code>, <code>updateLcd</code>, <code>updateKLine</code>, <code>_twCount</code> and <code>_cendCount</code>. Sections 5 to 11 state what each of these members does. The core is built with the defaults in §12; the adapter states <code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code> (the core's own default; until 28 Oct 2026 it overrode it with \"external\").</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s2\">2. Registers and memory</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Register</th><th>Width</th><th>Meaning / written by</th><th>ACL value (§6)</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Acc</td><td>4</td><td>accumulator</td><td>0</td><td>width ADJUDICATED [90] p170; reset value NOT ESTABLISHED, core chooses 0</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>carry. Written only by ADD11, ROT, RC, SC. <b>ADD and ADX never write C.</b></td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 rows</td></tr><tr><td>B_M</td><td>3</td><td>RAM file (X Y Z M P Q R S = 0..7)</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p165 Fig. 3</td></tr><tr><td>B_L</td><td>4</td><td>word within file</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p165 Fig. 3</td></tr><tr><td>W′</td><td>8</td><td><b>shift register</b>, written by WR/WS</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p163; see <code>W_PRIME_EXISTS_511</code></td></tr><tr><td>W</td><td>8</td><td><b>latch</b> that drives S1–S8 (the key-matrix strobes). Written only by PTW (W ← W′)</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p163</td></tr><tr><td>L</td><td>4</td><td>b_S1 flash source, written by ATL</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p163 §(4)</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>4</td><td>b_S2 flash source, written by ATX</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p163 §(4), [86] p95</td></tr><tr><td>Y</td><td>4</td><td>written by ATFC. Has no observable effect in this core</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 (Y ← Acc); destination not modelled</td></tr><tr><td>BP</td><td>4 stored; <b>only bit 0 is used</b></td><td>display-mode flip-flop, written by ATBP (stores Acc &amp; 0xF)</td><td><b>1</b></td><td>see <code>BP_ONE_FF_511</code>, <code>DISPLAY_ON_AT_ACL_511</code></td></tr><tr><td>BC</td><td>1</td><td>blanking flip-flop, written by BDC (BC ← C)</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p163 §(5): display mode needs BC = 0</td></tr><tr><td>ME</td><td>1</td><td>melody enable (gates the R pin only)</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 (SME/RME)</td></tr><tr><td>MES</td><td>1</td><td>melody stop flag (set by the engine, tested and cleared by TMEL)</td><td>0 on first power-on, <b>kept</b> across later ACLs</td><td>§11; <code>MES_SOURCE_511</code></td></tr><tr><td>melody address</td><td>8</td><td>melody memory pointer, loaded by PRE</td><td>0 on first power-on, kept across later ACLs</td><td><code>MELODY_POINTER_WIDTH_511</code></td></tr><tr><td>gamma (γ)</td><td>1</td><td>one-second flag</td><td>0</td><td>§8</td></tr><tr><td>divider</td><td>15</td><td>crystal counter, 0..32767</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(4) (15 stages)</td></tr><tr><td>K latch / K pending / wake line</td><td>4 / 4 / 1</td><td>§10</td><td>§10</td><td>§10</td></tr><tr><td>SBM latch, pending skip, previous-was-LAX</td><td>1 each</td><td>internal sequencing state</td><td>false</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>P_U, P_M, P_L</td><td>2, 4, 6</td><td>program counter</td><td>3, 7, 0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(1), §(2)</td></tr><tr><td>S, R (stack)</td><td>12 each</td><td>two return levels</td><td>both (0,0,0)</td><td>depth ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(1); reset value chosen</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>RAM.</b> 8 files × 16 words × 4 bits = 128 nibbles. The flat nibble index is <code>B_M × 16 + B_L</code>, so file Q is 0x50–0x5F, R is 0x60–0x6F and S is 0x70–0x7F. ADJUDICATED [90] p165 Fig. 3. The ABI's <code>ram</code> member is this array itself, not a copy, so a write from the site lands in the core. On SM511 the display files are R and S. On SM512 they are Q, R and S. On both parts the program can read and write every file, display files included, as ordinary RAM. ADJUDICATED [90] p165 Fig. 3 captions, [90] p169.</p>\n<p><b>Effective RAM address.</b> An instruction that touches M (the nibble at B) uses <code>B_M | 0b100</code> instead of B_M when the SBM latch is armed for that instruction (§4, <code>SBM</code>). Otherwise it uses B_M. The B_M register itself is never changed by SBM.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s3\">3. Program counter and program memory layout</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Layout.</b> There are 64 pages (P_U:P_M = 0x00..0x3F) of 63 steps each, for <b>4,032 words</b>. ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(2) and Fig. 1 (\"1 page consists of 63 steps\"). The byte for (P_U, P_M, P_L) is at image offset <code>(P_U&lt;&lt;10)|(P_M&lt;&lt;6)|P_L</code>. The ABI's <code>pc</code> member returns exactly that 12-bit value.</li><li><b>P_L is a 6-bit polynomial counter</b> [90] p164 §(2). The polynomial is NOT PRINTED for this part (<code>PL_RULE_511</code>) (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT sm511|sm-511|sm512|sm-512 ; polynomial|p_l.). <b>The core's rule is a right shift with XNOR feedback of bits 0 and 1:</b> the new P_L is <code>(P_L &gt;&gt; 1) | (NOT(P_L_0 XOR P_L_1) &lt;&lt; 5)</code>. This gives one 63-state cycle plus a lock state, 0x3F, whose only successor is itself. [SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE] <em>Reason:</em> <code>tools/sm511_lockstep.mjs</code> compares PC with the reference port after every slot, and the PCs agree on 27 of 27 titles over 300,000 slots under this rule. The printed structure (63 states) is enforced: a rule that does not give one 63-cycle plus the fixed point 0x3F refuses to construct.</li><li><b>The sequence from P_L = 0</b> (63 states, then back to 0x00). Every instruction that is not a jump advances P_L along this sequence by its own width:\n<p><code>00 20 30 38 3c 3e 1f 2f 37 3b 3d 1e 0f 27 33 39 1c 2e 17 2b 35 1a 0d 06 03 21 10 28 34 3a 1d 0e 07 23 31 18 2c 36 1b 2d 16 0b 25 12 09 04 22 11 08 24 32 19 0c 26 13 29 14 2a 15 0a 05 02 01</code> → <code>00</code></p></li><li><b>Stepping never leaves the page.</b> P_U and P_M change only through jumps. [MECHANISM; rule ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(2): \"for the instructions except for a jump instruction, P_L is shifted by 1 step\"]</li><li><b>Lock.</b> If P_L = 0x3F at the start of a slot, the slot's ticks still elapse (§5). The instruction is not executed, the PC stays where it is, and <code>step()</code> returns a <code>stopped: \"p_l-locked\"</code> result. This repeats on every later step. [MECHANISM] Only a T or computed jump to step 0x3F can reach this state. The lock test comes <b>before</b> the pending skip is looked at (§5 step 2.3 before 2.4), so a pending skip stays pending and is never retired. [MECHANISM: <code>src/cpu.mjs</code> <code>step()</code>. The order is NOT ESTABLISHED: no corpus image reaches 0x3F, so no run tells the two orders apart. SM510 uses the same order (10-SM510.md §5.1).]</li><li><b>Jumps</b> load fields directly and do not shift P_L afterwards: T, TL, TML, TM (via IDX), RTN0, RTN1 and ATPL. [Rule ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(2)]</li><li><b>Stack.</b> Push (TM, TML) does R ← S, then S ← the return address. Pop (RTN0, RTN1) does PC ← S, then S ← R. <b>R keeps its value after a pop.</b> [MECHANISM from [90] p170 \"P_U ← S_U ← R_U ...\"]</li><li><b>Subroutine cover page</b> = page P_U = 0, P_M = 0. It holds one-byte IDX words, not code (§4, TM). [MECHANISM; the page is ADJUDICATED [90] p164 Fig. 1, p165 Fig. 2] If the PC ever reaches this page, an event is recorded and execution continues normally. Nothing observable changes.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s4\">4. Instruction set</h4>\n<p>All encodings below are the <b>first byte</b>. Two-byte instructions take their operand from the next polynomial step on the same page. <b>The decoder in use is the printed table plus the source-read rows</b> (<code>sourceReadOps: true</code>, the default), and under that decoder <b>no first byte is unlisted.</b> The widths are: two bytes for <code>0x5F</code>, <code>0x60</code>, <code>0x61</code>, <code>0x68</code>–<code>0x6B</code> and <code>0x70</code>–<code>0x7F</code>; one byte for everything else. Time is in slots. Every row costs <b>1 slot = <code>clkDiv</code> ticks</b> (4 ticks after ACL, or 2 once the fast clock is selected). A skip costs one <b>extra</b> slot (§5). \"M\" means the RAM nibble at the effective address (§2).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Encoding</th><th>Mnemonic</th><th>Width</th><th>Operation (exact)</th><th>Skip</th><th>Time</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>0x00</code></td><td><code>ROT</code></td><td>1</td><td>new C ← Acc_0. Acc ← (Acc &gt;&gt; 1) | (old C &lt;&lt; 3). This is a right rotate through C.</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x01</code></td><td><code>DTA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← divider bits 14..11, i.e. <code>(count &gt;&gt; 11) &amp; 0xF</code> (f1 = bit 14 → Acc_3 ... f4 = bit 11 → Acc_0)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>SOURCE-READ (another emulator's source, which calls it \"guessed\"). Reason: NOT ESTABLISHED. The bit numbering follows SM500's printed DTA ([90] p126–127), a cross-part analogy. Executed 34,323 times in 16 titles, and the lockstep agrees</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x02</code></td><td><code>SBM</code></td><td>1</td><td>arms the SBM latch. The <b>next executed instruction</b> addresses RAM with B_M2 forced to 1. B_M is not written</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 \"B_M2 ← 1 (only next step)\"; modelling it as an addressing override is MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x03</code></td><td><code>ATPL</code></td><td>1</td><td>P_L ← (P_L of this ATPL &amp; 0x30) | Acc. P_U and P_M are unchanged. <b>No step afterwards</b>: the next instruction is at exactly that address</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 (P_L3–P_L0 ← Acc) + p164 §(2) (a jump)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x04</code>–<code>0x07</code></td><td><code>RM</code></td><td>1</td><td>bit (code &amp; 3) of M ← 0</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x08</code></td><td><code>ADD</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← (Acc + M) &amp; 0xF. C is untouched</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x09</code></td><td><code>ADD11</code></td><td>1</td><td>sum = Acc + M + C. C ← (sum &gt; 15). Acc ← sum &amp; 0xF</td><td>skip if the new C = 1</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x0A</code></td><td><code>COMA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← NOT Acc (4 bits)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x0B</code></td><td><code>EXBLA</code></td><td>1</td><td>swap Acc and B_L</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x0C</code>–<code>0x0F</code></td><td><code>SM</code></td><td>1</td><td>bit (code &amp; 3) of M ← 1</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 (the printed \"← I\" is read as 1; <code>SM_LITERAL_511</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x10</code>–<code>0x13</code></td><td><code>EXC</code></td><td>1</td><td>swap Acc and M (at the effective address). Then B_M ← B_M XOR (code &amp; 3) on the real B_M</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x14</code>–<code>0x17</code></td><td><code>EXCI</code></td><td>1</td><td>as EXC. Then skip ← (B_L = 15), and B_L ← (B_L + 1) &amp; 0xF</td><td>skip if B_L was 15</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x18</code>–<code>0x1B</code></td><td><code>LDA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← M (<b>a load, not an exchange</b>). Then B_M ← B_M XOR (code &amp; 3)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>MECHANISM (the mnemonic is LoaD; p170's printed \"↔\" is treated as an erratum, <code>LDA_ARROW_511</code>). Corroborated by the lockstep, 27 of 27 (CONVERGENCE)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x1C</code>–<code>0x1F</code></td><td><code>EXCD</code></td><td>1</td><td>as EXC. Then skip ← (B_L = 0), and B_L ← (B_L − 1) &amp; 0xF</td><td>skip if B_L was 0</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x20</code>–<code>0x2F</code></td><td><code>LAX</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← code &amp; 0xF, <b>unless the previous executed instruction was also a LAX</b>, in which case nothing happens. The first LAX of a run loads (§5)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE, <code>LAX_SUCCESSION_511</code>. Immediate = I3–I0 (MECHANISM: 16 encodings, 4-bit Acc)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x30</code>–<code>0x3F</code> (not <code>0x3A</code>)</td><td><code>ADX</code></td><td>1</td><td>sum = Acc + (code &amp; 0xF). Acc ← sum &amp; 0xF. <b>C is untouched</b></td><td>skip if sum &gt; 15</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x3A</code></td><td><code>DC</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← (Acc + 10) &amp; 0xF. C is untouched</td><td><b>never skips</b></td><td>1</td><td>MECHANISM: p170 prints DC as its own row with no skip, and the alias row wins (<code>DC_SKIP_BEHAVIOUR_511</code>). Corroborated by the lockstep (CONVERGENCE)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x40</code>–<code>0x4F</code></td><td><code>LB</code></td><td>1</td><td>B_L1,B_L0 ← I3,I2. B_M1,B_M0 ← I1,I0. <b>B_L3 and B_L2 both ← (I3 OR I2).</b> B_M2 is unchanged. So B_L is one of {0, 13, 14, 15}</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>fields: MECHANISM (<code>LB_OPERAND_FIELDS_511</code>); high bits: SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE (<code>LB_HIGH_BITS_511</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x50</code></td><td><code>KTA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← K latch (4 bits, §10)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x51</code></td><td><code>TB</code></td><td>1</td><td>test β (the <code>inB</code> pin)</td><td>skip if β = 1</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x52</code></td><td><code>TC</code></td><td>1</td><td>test C</td><td>skip if <b>C = 0</b></td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x53</code></td><td><code>TAM</code></td><td>1</td><td>compare</td><td>skip if Acc = M</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x54</code>–<code>0x57</code></td><td><code>TMI</code></td><td>1</td><td>test bit (code &amp; 3) of M</td><td>skip if that bit = 1</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x58</code></td><td><code>TIS</code></td><td>1</td><td>skip ← (γ = 0). Then γ ← 0, <b>unconditionally</b></td><td>skip if γ was 0</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 (the \"IS\" is read as 1S, <code>TIS_OPERAND_511</code>); the unconditional clear is MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x59</code></td><td><code>ATL</code></td><td>1</td><td>L ← Acc</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x5A</code></td><td><code>TA0</code></td><td>1</td><td>test Acc</td><td>skip if Acc = 0</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x5B</code></td><td><code>TABL</code></td><td>1</td><td>compare</td><td>skip if Acc = B_L</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x5C</code></td><td><code>ATX</code></td><td>1</td><td>X ← Acc</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x5D</code></td><td><code>CEND</code></td><td>1</td><td>halt: the CPU clock stops, and the divider and melody keep running (§7)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>encoding SOURCE-READ. Reason: it is the hole exactly where SM510 keeps CEND. The instruction's existence is ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(5), p164 Fig. 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x5E</code></td><td><code>TAL</code></td><td>1</td><td>test BA (the <code>inBA</code> pin)</td><td>skip if BA = 1</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x5F</code></td><td><code>LBL</code></td><td>2</td><td>operand byte b: B_M ← (b &gt;&gt; 4) &amp; 7, B_L ← b &amp; 0xF (b's bit 7 is ignored)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60</code></td><td><code>PFX60</code></td><td>2</td><td>prefix: the operand byte selects one of the rows below. Any other operand byte is a <b>no-operation</b> that still consumes both bytes and one slot, and is counted as an undefined prefix</td><td>none (except TMEL)</td><td>1</td><td>MECHANISM (<code>PREFIX_60_IS_ONE_ENCODING</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x30</code></td><td><code>RME</code></td><td>2</td><td>ME ← 0</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x31</code></td><td><code>SME</code></td><td>2</td><td>ME ← 1</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x32</code></td><td><code>TMEL</code></td><td>2</td><td>skip ← (MES = 1). Then MES ← 0, unconditionally</td><td>skip if MES was 1</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x33</code></td><td><code>ATFC</code></td><td>2</td><td>Y ← Acc</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x34</code></td><td><code>BDC</code></td><td>2</td><td>BC ← C (C = 1 blanks the display)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170, p163 §(5) (<code>BDC_NOTATION_511</code>: printed \"60-34\")</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x35</code></td><td><code>ATBP</code></td><td>2</td><td>BP ← Acc &amp; 0xF. Only bit 0 matters (§9)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170; the bit used is SOURCE-READ</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x36</code></td><td><code>CLKHI</code></td><td>2</td><td>selects the 16.384 kHz system clock: <code>clkDiv</code> ← 2 from the <b>next</b> slot</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>selection ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(6); encoding SOURCE-READ. Reason: NOT ESTABLISHED. Executed in 19 titles, and the lockstep agrees</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60 0x37</code></td><td><code>CLKLO</code></td><td>2</td><td>selects the 8.192 kHz system clock: <code>clkDiv</code> ← 4 from the next slot</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>as CLKHI</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x60+0x00-0x2F</code>, <code>0x60+0x38-0xFF</code></td><td>(undefined)</td><td>2</td><td>no-operation, counted</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>MECHANISM (250 of 256 second bytes are not printed (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT sm511</td><td>sm-511</td><td>sm512</td><td>sm-512 ; instruction set</td><td>instruction</td><td>opcode.))</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x61</code></td><td><code>PRE</code></td><td>2</td><td>melody address ← operand byte (all 8 bits). The melody step counter ← 0. The duty counters are untouched</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>load ADJUDICATED [90] p170; width ADJUDICATED [86] p94; clearing the step counter is SOURCE-READ</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x62</code></td><td><code>WR</code></td><td>1</td><td>W′ ← (W′ &lt;&lt; 1) &amp; 0xFF, with 0 entering bit 0. <b>W (the strobes) does not change</b></td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>MECHANISM from [90] p163 (<code>WR_WS_SOURCE_511</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x63</code></td><td><code>WS</code></td><td>1</td><td>W′ ← ((W′ &lt;&lt; 1) | 1) &amp; 0xFF. W does not change</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>as WR</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x64</code></td><td><code>INCB</code></td><td>1</td><td>skip ← (B_L = 15). B_L ← (B_L + 1) &amp; 0xF</td><td>skip if B_L was 15</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x65</code></td><td><code>IDIV</code></td><td>1</td><td>divider count ← 0. γ is untouched and no γ edge happens. This also re-phases the melody time base (§11)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>encoding SOURCE-READ. Reason: NOT ESTABLISHED. [90] p159 F8 prints \"divider with reset\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x66</code></td><td><code>RC</code></td><td>1</td><td>C ← 0</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x67</code></td><td><code>SC</code></td><td>1</td><td>C ← 1</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x68</code>–<code>0x6B</code></td><td><code>TML</code></td><td>2</td><td>push (return = the step <b>two</b> after TML, i.e. past the operand). P_U ← b &gt;&gt; 6. P_M ← code &amp; 3 (so P_M3,P_M2 = 0). P_L ← b &amp; 0x3F</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x6C</code></td><td><code>DECB</code></td><td>1</td><td>skip ← (B_L = 0). B_L ← (B_L − 1) &amp; 0xF</td><td>skip if B_L was 0</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x6D</code></td><td><code>PTW</code></td><td>1</td><td>W ← W′ (all 8 bits, in parallel). If W changes, the K pending value is re-sampled (§10)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>MECHANISM from [90] p163 (<code>PTW_DESTINATION_511</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x6E</code></td><td><code>RTN0</code></td><td>1</td><td>pop: PC ← S, S ← R</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x6F</code></td><td><code>RTN1</code></td><td>1</td><td>pop, then skip the instruction at the return address</td><td>always skips</td><td>1 (+1 for the skip)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x70</code>–<code>0x7F</code></td><td><code>TL</code></td><td>2</td><td>P_U ← b &gt;&gt; 6. P_M ← code &amp; 0xF. P_L ← b &amp; 0x3F</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0x80</code>–<code>0xBF</code></td><td><code>T</code></td><td>1</td><td>P_L ← code &amp; 0x3F (same page)</td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>0xC0</code>–<code>0xFF</code></td><td><code>TM</code></td><td>1</td><td>push (return = the step after TM). Read the IDX byte w at (P_U 0, P_M 0, P_L = code &amp; 0x3F). Then P_U ← w &gt;&gt; 6, <b>P_M ← 4</b>, P_L ← w &amp; 0x3F. <b>The whole dispatch is one slot</b></td><td>none</td><td>1</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170 TM/IDX row; the one-byte IDX word and one-slot cost are MECHANISM (<code>IDX_STRUCTURE_511</code>). <code>0xFF</code> reads the cover-page byte at P_L = 0x3F like any other (<code>TM_64TH_TARGET_511</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Removed from SM510 and not to be carried over:</b> <code>ATR</code> (0x61 is PRE here), <code>TF1</code>, <code>TF4</code>, and SM510's direct-strobe WR/WS. [ADJUDICATED [90] p170 (absent)] <b>The page count:</b> p170 prints 50 rows. With the prefix expanded and the alias DC counted there are 49 opcode mnemonics, against the \"55 instructions\" claimed on p159/p169 (erratum E-8). INSTRUMENT <code>spec/isa_sm511.mjs</code> partition control: 256 first bytes claimed exactly once, 6 prefix second bytes.</p>\n<p><b>Page-only machine (NOT SCORED).</b> <code>sourceReadOps: false</code> puts <code>0x01</code>, <code>0x5D</code> and <code>0x65</code> back as <b>unlisted</b>. An unlisted byte is recorded, advances one step, costs one slot, and does nothing else. <code>0x60 0x36</code>/<code>0x37</code> become undefined prefix no-operations. On this rival the card scores 15 of 27.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s5\">5. Execution model</h4>\n<p><b>One ABI <code>step()</code> call.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>If the machine is halted:</b> advance <code>clkDiv</code> ticks (each tick is described below). After the last of those ticks, check the wake condition (§7). If it does not wake, <code>step()</code> returns <code>null</code>. <b>If it wakes:</b> PC ← (1, 0, 0), halt is cleared, and the <b>same <code>step()</code> call</b> then runs one normal slot (2.) at the wake vector and returns that slot's result. A waking <code>step()</code> therefore spends two slots of crystal. [CONVERGENCE] Reason: the site engine paces frames by counting <code>step()</code> calls. Without this convention only 3 of 27 titles stayed frame-identical (board §26.3 G4).</li><li><b>A normal slot</b>, in this order:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Commit the K latch: K latch ← K pending (§10).</li><li><b>Advance <code>clkDiv</code> ticks first, then act</b> (<code>SLOT_PHASE_511</code> \"before\"). <code>clkDiv</code> is the value in force <b>when the slot starts</b>, so a CLKHI/CLKLO takes effect from the next slot. Each tick is: the divider increments, which may set γ (§8); then the melody engine ticks with the new divider count and the current ME (§11), which may set MES.</li><li>If P_L = 0x3F, stop (§3) and return the stopped result.</li><li><b>If a skip is pending:</b> fetch the first byte at the PC and look up its width (1 or 2) in the decoder. Advance the PC by that many polynomial steps. Clear the pending skip and the previous-was-LAX flag. Do nothing else: no SBM consumption, no RAM access. This is a whole slot that <b>retires the skipped instruction as a no-operation</b>. The result's mnemonic is <code>SKIP</code>.</li><li><b>Otherwise execute:</b> fetch the first byte, plus the operand from PC+1 if the instruction is 2 bytes wide. Set the SBM override for this instruction ← the armed SBM latch, then disarm it. Execute the row in §4. The next PC is the jump target if the row jumps, otherwise PC advanced by the width. If the row's skip condition holds, set the pending skip. Set previous-was-LAX ← (this was LAX).</li><li>If W changed in this instruction, re-sample K pending, and re-latch the wake line from it (<code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code>, §10).</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p><b>Skip rules.</b> A skip skips one whole instruction, operand byte included. An operand byte is never decoded as an opcode. The skip is performed by the next slot, which costs one slot of crystal. [MECHANISM] Reason: the width can only be learned by fetching and decoding, which is an instruction cycle. The cost is not printed anywhere (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT sm510|sm511 ; skip|instruction cycle.). RTN1's skip applies at the return address. A skipped SBM does not arm the latch, and a skipped LAX does not count as a LAX predecessor.</p>\n<p><b>LAX succession.</b> \"Previous\" means the previous <b>executed</b> instruction. A skip-retire slot resets the flag. A halted slot leaves it unchanged, but the instruction before any halt is CEND, so the flag is false after a wake anyway.</p>\n<p><b>Timing summary.</b> One slot is <code>clkDiv</code> = 4 ticks at 8.192 kHz (the clock after ACL) or 2 ticks at 16.384 kHz [MECHANISM: 32,768 / 8,192 and 32,768 / 16,384; the clocks are ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(6)]. Every instruction costs one slot, whatever its width [MECHANISM; [90] p169 prints a single 61 µs instruction cycle, which is 2 ticks at the fast clock].</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s6\">6. Reset (ACL) state</h4>\n<p>The ABI's <code>reset()</code> performs a power-on, then refreshes the K line (§10) and the LCD plane.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>After <code>reset()</code></th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PC</td><td>(3, 7, 0), so <code>pc</code> = 0xDC0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(2), p166 §(7)</td></tr><tr><td>Stack S, R</td><td>(0, 0, 0) each</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (not printed); core chooses 0</td></tr><tr><td>RAM</td><td>all 128 nibbles 0</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED; core chooses 0</td></tr><tr><td>B_M, B_L</td><td>0, 0</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED; core chooses 0</td></tr><tr><td>Acc, C, L, X, Y, W, W′</td><td>0</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED; core chooses 0</td></tr><tr><td>BP</td><td><b>1</b></td><td><code>DISPLAY_ON_AT_ACL_511</code></td></tr><tr><td>BC</td><td>0 (display mode)</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED; core chooses the printed display-mode value</td></tr><tr><td>ME</td><td>0</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED; core chooses 0</td></tr><tr><td>MES</td><td><b>0 on the first power-on. On every later <code>reset()</code> it keeps its pre-reset value</b></td><td>SOURCE-READ (another emulator's device reset touches only ME) + CONVERGENCE. Reason: the site driver's demo entry resets with Time held, and the program then runs TMEL straight after ACL. 16 titles took the other branch when this core cleared MES (board §28.2). Named <code>MELODY_ACROSS_ACL_511</code> in the core's notes; it is <b>not</b> a <code>CHOICES_511</code> key and has no option (§12, \"Other options\")</td></tr><tr><td>melody address, step counter, duty index, duty counter, pin level</td><td><b>0 on the first power-on. Kept unchanged on later resets</b></td><td>same reason</td></tr><tr><td>divider count, γ</td><td>0, 0</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED; core chooses 0 (the divider is re-created)</td></tr><tr><td>system clock</td><td><b>8.192 kHz: <code>clkDiv</code> = 4</b>, even if CLKHI was in force</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(6): \"The system clock immediately after executing ACL is set to 8.192kHz\"</td></tr><tr><td>halted</td><td>false</td><td>MECHANISM (ACL starts execution, [90] p166 §(7))</td></tr><tr><td>SBM latch, pending skip, previous-was-LAX</td><td>false</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>K latch, K pending, wake line</td><td><b>not cleared by ACL</b>. <code>reset()</code> then re-samples K pending and the wake line (§10) with W = 0</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>0.5 s ACL settling</td><td><b>not applied</b> (zero ticks elapse in <code>reset()</code>)</td><td>adapter choice; the 0.5 s is ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(7), but the <code>settle</code> option is off</td></tr><tr><td>β and BA pins (<code>inB</code>, <code>inBA</code>)</td><td><b>read 1 (pull-up) always</b>, under the default <code>hammerPins511: \"pulled-up\"</code>: a write is accepted and ignored (§10), and <code>reset()</code> does not touch the core's pins, which stay 1. Only under the rival <code>\"site\"</code> (NOT SCORED) do the core's pins keep the last value written, through <code>reset()</code> as well. <em>(Corrected 2026-10-04, found by a clean-room build: this row said they \"keep the last value written\", which is the rival's behaviour, and read against §10 as a contradiction.)</em></td><td>MECHANISM <code>adapter/site-abi.mjs</code>: the <code>inB</code>/<code>inBA</code> setters write the core's pin only under <code>\"site\"</code>, the getters read the core's pin. MEASURED 2026-10-04 on the reference, SM511 and SM512: fresh <code>1, 1</code>; after writing 0 to both, <code>1, 1</code>; after <code>reset()</code>, <code>1, 1</code>. <code>reset()</code> rebuilds the CPU and timing but not the pin object, which the constructor sets to 1 ([90] p161 pull-up, ADJUDICATED). SOURCE-READ: another emulator's device reset does not touch them either; they are input lines read on demand. This differs from SM5A, whose <code>reset()</code> sets them to 1 (20-SM5A.md §6). Not observable on the site, which writes <code>inB</code>/<code>inBA</code> before every frame's steps</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The \"first power-on\" is the first <code>reset()</code> after construction.</p>\n<p><b><code>step()</code> before any <code>reset()</code>.</b> The reference throws a plain <code>Error</code> whose message is <code>\"sm511: stepped before powerOn()\"</code> (on SM512 too), and changes nothing. It is not an <code>AbiRefusal</code>. [MECHANISM: <code>src/sm511.mjs</code> <code>stepInstruction()</code>; SM510 behaves the same with <code>\"sm510: ...\"</code>, and SM5A does not throw, because its constructor already holds the ACL state.] A build <b>need not</b> raise it: the site always calls <code>reset()</code> first (60-ABI.md §1) and no acceptance section steps an unreset CPU, so either behaviour passes. NOT SCORED.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s7\">7. Standby, halt and wake</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>CEND (<code>0x5D</code>)</b> sets halted. Because of the slot order (§5), the ticks of CEND's own slot have already elapsed. <code>_cendCount</code> increments on each CEND executed.</li><li><b>While halted</b>, every <code>step()</code> advances <code>clkDiv</code> ticks. The divider <b>and the melody engine</b> keep running, so MES can be raised during a halt. [MECHANISM from [90] p166 §(5): CEND halts \"almost all the system clocks\", and γ, the divider's own 1S output, restarts the part] <code>halt</code> reads true.</li><li><b>Wake condition</b>, checked once at the end of each halted slot's ticks: <b>γ = 1, or the wake line is high</b> (<code>kWake: \"level\"</code>). [ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(5): \"γ F/F must be reset <b>or</b> one or more inputs of K1–K4 must go High\"] The level reading of \"go High\" is NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>K_WAKE_EDGE_511</code>, a name in the core's notes, not a <code>CHOICES_511</code> key; the option is <code>kWake</code>, rival <code>\"edge\"</code>): the card scores 27 of 27 under both readings. Note what follows from the level reading. If γ is already 1 when CEND executes, or the wake line is already high, the very next slot wakes.</li><li><b>Waking</b> sets PC ← (1, 0, 0) (\"Start from CEND\", ADJUDICATED [90] p164 Fig. 1, p166 §(5)) and clears halted. <b>γ is NOT cleared by waking</b>. Only TIS clears it.</li><li><b>At the ABI a wake found by a halted slot is taken inside <code>step()</code></b>, together with the first instruction at the vector (§5.1). The ABI's <code>updateKLine()</code> re-latches the wake line and, when that raises it on a halted part, <b>wakes the part at once</b>; the next <code>step()</code> runs the instruction at the vector. [MECHANISM, as SM510 <code>10-SM510.md</code> §7.3] Rival (NOT SCORED): under <code>wakeRelatch: \"external\"</code> (the old site engine's), <code>updateKLine()</code> does not wake the machine itself.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s8\">8. Divider and the one-second flag</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The divider is a 15-bit count, 0..32767. It increments once per tick and wraps. [ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(4) (15 stages); crystal 32.768 kHz, [90] p161]</li><li><b>γ ← 1 on the tick at which the count wraps from 32767 to 0</b>, i.e. 32,768 ticks (one second) after ACL or IDIV (<code>GAMMA_PHASE_511</code>, <code>gammaEdge: \"wrap\"</code>). IDIV setting the count to 0 is <b>not</b> an edge.</li><li>One second is 8,192 slots at 8.192 kHz and 16,384 slots at 16.384 kHz [MECHANISM].</li><li><b>DTA</b> reads <code>(count &gt;&gt; 11) &amp; 0xF</code> (§4). <b>IDIV</b> sets the count to 0.</li><li>The divider does not depend on the selected system clock. It counts crystal ticks. [ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(6): \"the rest of functions are the same\"]</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s9\">9. Display</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Display enable</b> = (BC = 0) AND (BP bit 0 = 1). When this is false, every segment and flash output reads 0 on all four commons. [ADJUDICATED [90] p163 §(5) (BP = 1 and BC = 0; \"BP F/F ... display mode or blanking mode\", one flip-flop); see <code>BP_ONE_FF_511</code>]</li><li><b>Segments.</b> For common h (0..3) and word w (0..15) of a display file, the segment is lit when the display is enabled and bit h of RAM[file × 16 + w] is 1. [ADJUDICATED [90] p168 §(9) (1/4 duty)] On SM511 the files are R and S. On SM512 they are Q, R and S.</li><li><b>Flash pins.</b> b_S1 on common h = enabled AND bit h of <b>L</b>. b_S2 on common h = enabled AND bit h of <b>X</b>. [Sources ADJUDICATED [90] p163 §(4); the same-common phase for b_S2 is <code>BS2_PHASE</code>]</li><li><b>The plane at the ABI.</b> <code>lcd</code> is a <code>Uint16Array</code>, 12 entries long on SM511 and 16 on SM512. It is indexed <code>(A &lt;&lt; 2) | h</code>, where bit w of each entry is word w. <b>A is assigned by FILE, not by lane order</b>:\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>content</th><th>part</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>file R (RAM 0x60–0x6F)</td><td>both</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>file S (RAM 0x70–0x7F)</td><td>both</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>flash: bit 0 = b_S1 (L), bit 1 = b_S2 (X)</td><td>both</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>file Q (RAM 0x50–0x5F)</td><td>SM512 only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>[TABLE-READ (the site's <code>updateLcd()</code> naming)] Reason: the site artwork names its segment ids in this space, so a lane-order mapping would paint SM512's file Q onto A = 0.</p></li><li>The plane is recomputed from the current RAM, BP, BC, L and X only when <code>updateLcd()</code> or <code>reset()</code> is called. The core keeps no LCD timing state. <code>r</code> is a constant 0 on this part, which has no R latch.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s10\">10. Input</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Pins.</b> β (<code>inB</code>) and BA (<code>inBA</code>) default to 1 (pull-up). K1–K4 pull down. [ADJUDICATED [90] p161] TB tests β and TAL tests BA, live.</li><li><b>B and BA stay pulled up at the site surface.</b> The ABI accepts writes to <code>inB</code> and <code>inBA</code> and ignores them; the getters read the core's real pin, which stays 1. No G&amp;W title wires either pin to a button: on these boards they are factory-test cheat jumpers [SOURCE-READ, another emulator's G&amp;W board drivers]. The site drives both from its left/right hotspots on every title, so forwarding them would let a player's press close a cheat. Switch: <code>hammerPins511</code>, default <code>\"pulled-up\"</code>; rival <code>\"site\"</code> forwards the writes (NOT SCORED). [MECHANISM <code>adapter/site-abi.mjs</code>; INSTRUMENT <code>tools/sm511_parity_cause.mjs</code>]</li><li><b>The factory test contacts have their own wire</b> (2026-10-28). The extra, non-enumerable member <code>testContacts</code> (60-ABI.md §3) drives β and BA: <code>{ B, BA }</code> with 0 = the pad closed, <code>null</code> = both back to 1. It is the only way the site closes a contact on these parts, so a player's left/right press still never does. Closed after a game has started, the pads give the reference's result on 26 of the 33 contacts of these units; on the other 7 the reference shows no effect to compare with (a switch sweep with the same drive on both, 2026-10-28). [ADJUDICATED pull-up and TB/TAL [90] p161; the pads as B/BA SOURCE-READ; the effects CONVERGENCE]</li><li><b>Wiring at the ABI.</b> If <code>inFixedRow</code> ≥ 0, the direct K value ← <code>inRows[inFixedRow]</code> &amp; 0xF and the matrix is all zeros. Otherwise matrix[i] ← <code>inRows[i]</code> &amp; 0xF for i = 0..7, and the direct value is 0. Setting <code>inRows</code>/<code>inFixedRow</code> only rewires; it does <b>not</b> re-sample. [MECHANISM]</li><li><b>Sampled K</b> = direct K OR (matrix[i] for every i where bit i of <b>W</b>, the latch, is 1). The strobes are W, not W′. [ADJUDICATED [90] p163 §(1)]</li><li><b>Three stored values:</b> K pending, K latch and the wake line.\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The ABI's <code>updateKLine()</code> and <code>reset()</code> set K pending ← sampled K, and wake line ← (sampled K ≠ 0).</li><li>An instruction that changes W (only PTW can) sets K pending ← sampled K <b>and the wake line</b> ← (sampled K ≠ 0) (<code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code>, MECHANISM: [90] p154 s(5) wakes on the K INPUTS, and a strobe that selects a held key raises one; matched to the reference emulator's outputs, <code>10-SM510.md</code> §7.3). Rival (NOT SCORED): \"external\", the old site engine's convention, where only <code>updateKLine()</code> and <code>reset()</code> re-latch the wake line.</li><li>At the start of every non-halted slot, K latch ← K pending (§5).</li></ul></li><li><b>KTA</b> reads the K latch. In practice, a KTA in the slot right after a PTW reads the new column, and a site input change is seen from the next slot after <code>updateKLine()</code>. (<code>K_LINE_SAMPLE_POINT</code> \"latched\", MECHANISM; the rival \"live\" is NOT SCORED.)</li><li><code>_twCount</code> counts the WR and WS instructions executed. <code>rMuxInvert</code> must be false: setting it true throws, because the flag only applies to SM5A.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s11\">11. Sound</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">11.1 Where the engine runs</h5>\n<p>The melody engine runs off the <b>crystal</b>: once per tick, inside the slot's ticks (§5), including halted slots and skip slots. It does <b>not</b> run off the instruction slot. [ADJUDICATED [90] p166 Fig. 5 note (\"clock cycle count of the crystal oscillation frequency 32.768kHz\"); [86] p94 (the generator is fed from DIV)] Changing the system clock therefore changes neither pitch nor tempo.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">11.2 Melody memory word format</h5>\n<p>There are 256 words. Each word is memory byte &amp; 0x3F. [ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(8) (256 × 6)]</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bits</th><th>field</th><th>values</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3–0</td><td>code</td><td>2–13 = the twelve printed notes (§11.3). <b>0 = rest. 1 = stop. 14, 15 = silent, no flag</b></td><td>notes ADJUDICATED [90] p166 Fig. 5; 0, 1, 14 and 15 SOURCE-READ. Reason: Fig. 5 prints no tone for these four codes (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT sm511</td><td>sm-511</td><td>sm512</td><td>sm-512 ; pause.), and §(8) names a \"pause\" and a \"stop\". The corpus uses only 0 and 1 of the four (14 and 15 appear in 0 of 4,864 words). Which code is which is NOT ESTABLISHED beyond the source</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>octave (<code>BIT_UPPER_OCTAVE</code> 0x10)</td><td>1 = the printed (upper) octave. 0 = every half period doubled, one octave down</td><td>SOURCE-READ. Doubling is MECHANISM (\"two octaves\" with one table on the crystal grid). The <b>polarity is NOT ESTABLISHED</b></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>length (<code>BIT_LONG</code> 0x20)</td><td>1 = 32 time-base periods (125 ms). 0 = 16 periods (62.5 ms)</td><td>SOURCE-READ. The values are ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(8), and 16 × 128 = 2,048 and 32 × 128 = 4,096 ticks close them exactly (MECHANISM)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Pointer:</b> 8 bits, loaded whole by PRE, incrementing mod 256 (<code>MELODY_POINTER_WIDTH_511</code>).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">11.3 The tone table</h5>\n<p>Each note is two full cycles made of four half periods k = 0..3, measured in ticks. Let <code>S = 2 × (printed clock cycles)</code>. Then half period k = ⌊(k+1)S/4⌋ − ⌊kS/4⌋. [MECHANISM: this reproduces all 48 printed waveform numerics of [90] p166 Fig. 5] Frequency = 65,536 / S Hz (upper octave). The lower octave doubles every half period, so its frequency is 32,768 / S. The note's R level is <b>low during k = 0 and 2, and high during k = 1 and 3</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>code</th><th>note</th><th>printed cycles [90] p166</th><th>S (ticks)</th><th>half periods, bit 4 = 1</th><th>Hz, bit 4 = 1</th><th>half periods, bit 4 = 0</th><th>Hz, bit 4 = 0</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>do</td><td>15.5</td><td>31</td><td>7, 8, 8, 8</td><td>2114.06</td><td>14, 16, 16, 16</td><td>1057.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>si</td><td>16.5</td><td>33</td><td>8, 8, 8, 9</td><td>1985.94</td><td>16, 16, 16, 18</td><td>992.97</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>la#</td><td>17.5</td><td>35</td><td>8, 9, 9, 9</td><td>1872.46</td><td>16, 18, 18, 18</td><td>936.23</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>la</td><td>18.5</td><td>37</td><td>9, 9, 9, 10</td><td>1771.24</td><td>18, 18, 18, 20</td><td>885.62</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>so#</td><td>19.5</td><td>39</td><td>9, 10, 10, 10</td><td>1680.41</td><td>18, 20, 20, 20</td><td>840.21</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>so</td><td>21.0</td><td>42</td><td>10, 11, 10, 11</td><td>1560.38</td><td>20, 22, 20, 22</td><td>780.19</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>fa#</td><td>22.0</td><td>44</td><td>11, 11, 11, 11</td><td>1489.45</td><td>22, 22, 22, 22</td><td>744.73</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>fa</td><td>23.5</td><td>47</td><td>11, 12, 12, 12</td><td>1394.38</td><td>22, 24, 24, 24</td><td>697.19</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>mi</td><td>25.0</td><td>50</td><td>12, 13, 12, 13</td><td>1310.72</td><td>24, 26, 24, 26</td><td>655.36</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>re#</td><td>26.5</td><td>53</td><td>13, 13, 13, 14</td><td>1236.53</td><td>26, 26, 26, 28</td><td>618.26</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>re</td><td>28.0</td><td>56</td><td>14, 14, 14, 14</td><td>1170.29</td><td>28, 28, 28, 28</td><td>585.14</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>do#</td><td>29.5</td><td>59</td><td>14, 15, 15, 15</td><td>1110.78</td><td>28, 30, 30, 30</td><td>555.39</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>rest</td><td>none</td><td>none</td><td>pin 0</td><td>none</td><td>pin 0</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>stop</td><td>none</td><td>none</td><td>pin 0, raises MES</td><td>none</td><td>same</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>14, 15</td><td>(none)</td><td>none</td><td>none</td><td>pin 0</td><td>none</td><td>pin 0</td><td>none</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(The printed prose \"555 to 2097 Hz\" is a misprint of 2114, GWC-E13. The table is what the core uses.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">11.4 One engine tick (exact)</h5>\n<p>The engine state is: address (8 bits), step counter, duty index (0..3), duty counter, and pin level. Each tick runs after the divider has advanced to <code>count</code>:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Read word = melody[address] &amp; 0x3F. Code = word &amp; 0xF. (With no melody memory, the tick does nothing.)</li><li><b>If the code is a note (2–13):</b> the tick's output = ME ? (duty index &amp; 1) : 0. This is the level of the phase the tick <b>starts</b> in. Then duty counter += 1. Let len = the half period for (code, duty index), doubled if bit 4 = 0. If duty counter ≥ len, then duty counter ← 0 and duty index ← (duty index + 1) &amp; 3. <b>If the code is 0, 1, 14 or 15:</b> the output is 0 and the duty counters are frozen (not reset). If the code is 1, MES ← 1 at the end of this tick. <b>This happens on every tick the stop word is current, regardless of ME</b>, and also while halted.</li><li><b>Time base:</b> if count mod 128 = 0 (count 0, i.e. the wrap, included), then step counter ← (step counter + 1) mod (32 if word bit 5 else 16). If it became 0, address ← (address + 1) &amp; 0xFF. The tick that advances the address used the old word for its tone and its modulus. Because this rides the shared divider, IDIV re-phases it. [SOURCE-READ tap; MECHANISM closure]</li><li>Pin level ← this tick's output.</li></ol>\n<p>Notes. Moving to a new word <b>never</b> resets the duty counters, so they carry across words and across rests. The address keeps advancing through rests and stops: a stop word lasts its 16 or 32 periods like any other word, and then the next word plays. ME only gates the pin; all counters run whether or not ME is set. [SOURCE-READ, reason NOT ESTABLISHED]</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">11.5 What the program sees, and the R pin</h5>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b><code>rOut</code></b> = the pin level after the last tick of the most recent slot (0 or 1). It is read-only; writing it throws.</li><li><b>MES</b> is set by stop words (above) and cleared only by TMEL (<code>0x60 0x32</code>), which skips the next instruction when MES was 1. Because MES is re-raised on every tick while a stop word is current, a TMEL that clears it sees it set again a slot later, unless the program PREs away (self-check L).</li><li><b>The MES rule, settled (2026-10-03).</b> MES ← 1 on every <b>crystal tick</b> whose current word has code 1, <b>whether or not ME is set and whether or not the CPU is halted</b> (the divider and the engine run through CEND, §7). Nothing but TMEL clears it; ACL does not (§6). The address keeps advancing on the time base while ME = 0, so MES can rise with the melody off. SOURCE-READ (another emulator's melody clock, called from its divider timer once per tick, and its TMEL) + CONVERGENCE. Printed: only TMEL's own test-and-clear ([90] p170, ADJUDICATED); what sets MES is not printed (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: QUOTED: it restates <code>MES_SOURCE_511</code>'s registered claim in <code>src/sm511.mjs</code>, which carries the subject). This core already implements exactly this rule.</li><li>PRE sets the address and step counter to (operand, 0) mid-period. The next address advance comes after 16 or 32 more time-base edges of the global divider phase.</li></ul>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">11.6 Where this core and the reference port part, and what the acceptance suite expects</h5>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Pitch.</b> The reference port calls its melody clock once per <b>instruction</b> while its divider moves by <code>clkDiv</code>. Every note therefore plays clkDiv times too low: printed/port = <b>4.000 at 8.192 kHz and 2.000 at 16.384 kHz</b>, i.e. two octaves or one octave low. INSTRUMENT <code>tools/melody511_check.mjs</code>: the core sounds the printed pitch on 48 of 48 runs (12 notes × 2 octaves × both clocks), and the octave-flip control misses 48 of 48. The page counts crystal cycles (ADJUDICATED [90] p166 Fig. 5). <b>Do not copy the reference port.</b></li><li><b>Frozen tempo.</b> After a 16→8 kHz switch that leaves its divider at 2 mod 4, the reference port's <code>div mod 128 = 0</code> test never fires again and its melody address freezes (0 steps/s; ours 16). This is a site bug and is not copied.</li><li><b>Frame partings, traced (2026-10-03; supersedes \"MES parting\").</b> MES is a symptom, not a cause: both engines set it by the same rule. 11 titles (<code>boxing dkcirc dkjrp dkjrt mariocmt mbaway mmousep popeyep popeyet snoopyp snoopyt</code>) part because the <b>port's melody address freezes</b> (the frozen-tempo bug above): frozen one word short of a stop it never raises MES (<code>dkcirc</code>), frozen on one it re-raises MES after every TMEL (<code>boxing</code>). <code>pinball</code> and <code>sbuster</code> parted at a TAL because the site's right hotspot reached BA; the adapter now holds B and BA pulled up on this part (<code>hammerPins511</code>). INSTRUMENT <code>tools/site_parity.mjs</code>: <b>16 of 27</b> frame-identical; <code>tools/sm511_parity_cause.mjs</code>: 27 of 27 with the reference port's time base made per-tick in memory. The acceptance tests hold the builder to <b>this core</b>, not to the reference port, on the 11.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s12\">12. Registered choices</h4>\n<p>Every <code>CHOICES_511</code> key (14), then every <code>CHOICES_512</code> key (2), then the other construction options. The \"default\" column is what the ABI runs.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>default (scored)</th><th>rival(s), NOT SCORED</th><th>tier</th><th>reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>LB_OPERAND_FIELDS_511</code></td><td>B_L1,B_L0 ← I3,I2 and B_M1,B_M0 ← I1,I0</td><td><code>lbOperandLiteral</code>: B_M1 ← I2, I1 unused</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>p170 prints I_2 twice. Only I1,I0 lets the 16 encodings address distinct targets</td></tr><tr><td><code>LB_HIGH_BITS_511</code></td><td>B_L3, B_L2 ← (I3 OR I2). B_M2 is kept</td><td><code>lbHighBits: \"unchanged\"</code> (card 2 of 27). <code>\"sm510-xor\"</code> (SM510's fold)</td><td>SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE</td><td>Reason: lockstep 27 of 27 titles over 300,000 slots under OR, 0 of 27 under \"unchanged\" (every title splits at an LB or a LAX within 1,055 slots). Reason on silicon NOT ESTABLISHED</td></tr><tr><td><code>WR_WS_SOURCE_511</code></td><td>the shift is inside W′ (W′_{i+1} ← W′_i)</td><td><code>wrWsSource: \"unprimed\"</code>: shift W into W′</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>[90] p163 prints W′ as the shift register; p170's unprimed source is read as a typo</td></tr><tr><td><code>W_PRIME_EXISTS_511</code></td><td>two registers: W′ (shift) and W (latch); only PTW copies</td><td>one register, as [86] p94 draws it (WS/WR move the strobes directly)</td><td>MECHANISM ([90] p163)</td><td>[90] is the book that prints the PTW instruction that tells the two apart. [86] p94 disagrees. Settling it needs the bench</td></tr><tr><td><code>PTW_DESTINATION_511</code></td><td>W_i ← W′_i for all 8 bits</td><td><code>ptwLiteral</code>: only W_1 ← W′_0</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>[90] p163 says \"transferred by the PTW instruction in parallel\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>MELODY_POINTER_WIDTH_511</code></td><td>the PRE operand is kept whole, 8 bits</td><td>a narrower pointer</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p94 (melody memory, 256 x 6)</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>MELODY_WORD_DECODE_511</code></td><td>decoded as §11.2 (<code>melodyEngine: \"r17\"</code>)</td><td><code>melodyEngine: \"refuse\"</code>: no decoder, no tone, MES never set</td><td>SOURCE-READ (another emulator's melody clock)</td><td>Reason: per bit as in §11.2. The octave polarity and the assignment of codes 0/1 are NOT ESTABLISHED</td></tr><tr><td><code>MES_SOURCE_511</code></td><td>MES ← 1 on every tick whose word code is 1. Cleared only by TMEL</td><td>MES follows ME</td><td>SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE</td><td>Reason: the lockstep agrees with the reference port on MES at every slot of 27 titles over 300,000 slots. What sets MES is not printed (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT sm511</td><td>sm-511</td><td>sm512</td><td>sm-512 ; tmel.)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LAX_SUCCESSION_511</code></td><td>the first LAX of a run loads. A LAX after a LAX does not</td><td><code>laxSuccession: \"last\"</code> (card 0 of 27)</td><td>SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE</td><td>Reason: with \"last\", 21 of 27 titles split at a LAX within 924 slots. Offered as MECHANISM: p170's word is \"Skip\", and every other skip acts on the next step</td></tr><tr><td><code>GAMMA_PHASE_511</code></td><td>γ is set when the divider wraps, 32,768 ticks after IDIV/ACL (<code>gammaEdge: \"wrap\"</code>)</td><td><code>\"msb-rise\"</code>: bit 14 rising, 16,384 ticks (card 22 of 27)</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>Reason: IDIV re-phases the second when the time is set, and a first second of 0.5 s would gain half a second each time the watch is set. [90] p166 §(4) does not print which phase 1S has (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT sm511</td><td>sm-511</td><td>sm512</td><td>sm-512 ; 1s signal</td><td>1 sec.).</td></tr><tr><td><code>SLOT_PHASE_511</code></td><td>the slot's ticks happen <b>before</b> its instruction acts (<code>slotPhase: \"before\"</code>)</td><td><code>\"after\"</code></td><td>FITTED (placement) + CONVERGENCE</td><td>Reason: under \"after\", <code>boxing</code>'s TMEL saw the stop flag a slot late, an RTN0 ran, and the frames parted at frame 36. This is the same choice as SM510 and SM5A. The lockstep scores 27 of 27 under \"before\" and 0 of 27 with a double shift</td></tr><tr><td><code>DISPLAY_ON_AT_ACL_511</code></td><td>ACL leaves BP = 1, BC = 0</td><td><code>bpAtAcl: 0</code> (card 15 of 27)</td><td>MECHANISM from INSTRUMENT</td><td>Reason: 13 of 27 titles never execute ATBP in 300,000 slots and are shipped products with a picture.</td></tr><tr><td><code>BP_ONE_FF_511</code></td><td>all four commons follow BP bit 0 (<code>bpRule: \"one-ff\"</code>)</td><td><code>\"per-common\"</code>: bit h gates common h (card 0 of 27)</td><td>MECHANISM ([90] p163 §(5)). The bit used is SOURCE-READ</td><td>Reason: every SM511/512 title writes only 0 or 1 to BP</td></tr><tr><td><code>PL_RULE_511</code></td><td>right shift, feedback XNOR(P_L_0, P_L_1) into bit 5 (§3)</td><td>any of the other 11 rules with a printed structure (<code>plRule</code>)</td><td>SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE</td><td>Reason: the PCs match the reference port after every slot, 27 of 27 titles over 300,000 slots. Not printed for this part</td></tr><tr><td><code>BS2_PHASE</code></td><td>b_S2 shows bit h of X on common h, the same as b_S1 with L</td><td>b_S2 one common out of step</td><td>MECHANISM ([90] p163 §(4) symmetric)</td><td>Reason: the sources are printed and the alignment is not. <b>The rival is not built</b>: <code>src/sm512.mjs</code> names a <code>bs2Phase</code> switch that does not exist</td></tr><tr><td><code>Q_IS_NOT_SCRATCH_ON_SM512</code></td><td>the caller states the part. File Q is displayed only on SM512</td><td>infer the part from the image</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p165 Fig. 3, p169</td><td>Reason: no byte-level separator can exist (same program memory size and instruction set). Neither wrong choice throws</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Other options (fixed on the ABI path).</b> None of these is a <code>CHOICES_511</code> or <code>CHOICES_512</code> key: <code>K_WAKE_EDGE_511</code> and <code>MELODY_ACROSS_ACL_511</code> are names used in the core's notes, and the open readings listed last are the instruction table's own list of open readings, each settled as §4 says. <code>clock</code> / <code>ticksPerInstruction</code> (see §5; re-selected at ACL). <code>sourceReadOps: true</code> (§4). <code>coverPageIsIdx: true</code> (TM dispatches via the IDX word; the rival jumps into page 0 as code). <code>stopOnUnlisted: false</code>. <code>kSamplePoint: \"latched\"</code>. <code>kWake: \"level\"</code> (<code>K_WAKE_EDGE_511</code>; NOT ESTABLISHED, the card gives 27 under either). <code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code>, stated by the adapter (the core's own default; \"external\" until 28 Oct 2026). MES and melody counters kept across a non-first ACL (<code>MELODY_ACROSS_ACL_511</code>, §6). Open readings of the p170 transcription, resolved as in §4: <code>LDA_ARROW_511</code> (load), <code>DC_SKIP_BEHAVIOUR_511</code> (no skip), <code>TM_64TH_TARGET_511</code> (0xFF reads cover step 0x3F), <code>PREFIX_60_IS_ONE_ENCODING</code>, <code>BDC_NOTATION_511</code>, <code>PTW_SUBSCRIPT_511</code>, <code>SM_LITERAL_511</code>, <code>TIS_OPERAND_511</code>, <code>IDX_STRUCTURE_511</code>, <code>WHERE_CEND_WENT</code>, <code>WHERE_IDIV_WENT</code>, <code>CLOCK_SELECT_OPCODE_511</code>, <code>PL_POLYNOMIAL_511</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s13\">13. Constants</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>identifier</th><th>value</th><th>meaning</th><th>tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>POWER_ON_VECTOR_511</code></td><td>{pu 3, pm 7, pl 0}</td><td>ACL start address</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(2), p166 §(7)</td></tr><tr><td><code>HALT_WAKE_VECTOR_511</code></td><td>{pu 1, pm 0, pl 0}</td><td>wake-from-CEND address</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p164 Fig. 1, p166 §(5)</td></tr><tr><td><code>PIN_DEFAULTS_511</code></td><td>{beta 1, ba 1, k 0, acl 0}</td><td>unconnected pin levels</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p161</td></tr><tr><td><code>PAGES_511</code></td><td>64</td><td>pages</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p164 §(2)</td></tr><tr><td><code>STEPS_PER_PAGE_511</code></td><td>63</td><td>P_L states per page</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p164 Fig. 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>PROGRAM_WORDS_511</code></td><td>4032</td><td>usable program words</td><td>MECHANISM (64 × 63)</td></tr><tr><td><code>SYSTEM_CLOCK_HZ_511</code></td><td>{fast 16384, slow 8192}</td><td>selectable system clocks</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(6)</td></tr><tr><td><code>CLOCK_AFTER_ACL_511</code></td><td>\"slow\"</td><td>clock selected by ACL (<code>clkDiv</code> 4)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(6)</td></tr><tr><td><code>INSTRUCTION_CYCLE_US_511</code></td><td>61</td><td>µs per instruction at the fast clock</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p169</td></tr><tr><td><code>SOURCE_READ_OPS_511</code></td><td>DTA 0x01, CEND 0x5D, IDIV 0x65 (1 byte each)</td><td>first bytes not on p170</td><td>SOURCE-READ (§4)</td></tr><tr><td><code>SOURCE_READ_PREFIX_511</code></td><td>0x36 → CLKHI (fast), 0x37 → CLKLO (slow)</td><td>prefix second bytes not on p170</td><td>SOURCE-READ (§4)</td></tr><tr><td><code>SM511_DECODER</code></td><td>p170 only; unlisted {0x01, 0x5D, 0x65}</td><td>page-only decoder (NOT SCORED)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p170</td></tr><tr><td><code>SM511_DECODER_SR</code></td><td>p170 + <code>SOURCE_READ_OPS_511</code>; unlisted none</td><td>the scored decoder</td><td>SOURCE-READ rows</td></tr><tr><td><code>CHOICES_511</code></td><td>14 keys (§12)</td><td>registry</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>DISPLAY_FILES_511</code></td><td>[6, 7]</td><td>R, S</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p165 Fig. 3</td></tr><tr><td><code>DISPLAY_FILES_512</code></td><td>[5, 6, 7]</td><td>Q, R, S</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p165 Fig. 3</td></tr><tr><td><code>PRINTED_512</code></td><td>program memory 32,256 bits, 55 instructions, nesting 2, 6 inputs, 61 µs, RAM 80×4 + 48×4, 63 outputs, 200 segments, 80QFP</td><td>the p169 SM512 column</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p169</td></tr><tr><td><code>SM512_PROFILE</code></td><td>part \"SM512\", displayFiles [5,6,7], bsPins 2, segmentPins 50</td><td>the SM512 profile</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p161, p165, p169</td></tr><tr><td><code>CHOICES_512</code></td><td>2 keys (§12)</td><td>registry</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>TIME_BASE_TICKS</code></td><td>128</td><td>ticks per melody time-base period</td><td>SOURCE-READ (tap); MECHANISM closure</td></tr><tr><td><code>STEPS_SHORT</code></td><td>16</td><td>periods per word, bit 5 = 0 (62.5 ms = 2,048 ticks)</td><td>SOURCE-READ; length ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>STEPS_LONG</code></td><td>32</td><td>periods per word, bit 5 = 1 (125 ms = 4,096 ticks)</td><td>as above</td></tr><tr><td><code>WORD_MASK</code></td><td>0x3F</td><td>6-bit melody word</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>CODE_REST</code></td><td>0</td><td>rest code</td><td>SOURCE-READ</td></tr><tr><td><code>CODE_STOP</code></td><td>1</td><td>stop code (raises MES)</td><td>SOURCE-READ</td></tr><tr><td><code>BIT_UPPER_OCTAVE</code></td><td>0x10</td><td>1 = printed octave</td><td>SOURCE-READ, polarity NOT ESTABLISHED</td></tr><tr><td><code>BIT_LONG</code></td><td>0x20</td><td>1 = 125 ms</td><td>SOURCE-READ</td></tr><tr><td><code>PRINTED_CLOCK_CYCLES</code> (shared)</td><td>codes 2..13 → 15.5, 16.5, 17.5, 18.5, 19.5, 21.0, 22.0, 23.5, 25.0, 26.5, 28.0, 29.5</td><td>Fig. 5 cycles</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 Fig. 5</td></tr><tr><td><code>MELODY_STEPS</code> (shared)</td><td>256</td><td>melody words</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>MELODY_WORD_BITS</code> (shared)</td><td>6</td><td>melody word width</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>OSCILLATOR_HZ</code> (shared)</td><td>32768</td><td>crystal</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p161</td></tr><tr><td><code>DIVIDER_STAGES</code> (shared)</td><td>15</td><td>divider width</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 §(4)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LOCK_STATE</code> (shared)</td><td>0x3F</td><td>P_L lock</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>BS_PINS</code> (shared)</td><td>SM511 2, SM512 2</td><td>flash pins</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p161</td></tr><tr><td><code>LCD_C_BITS</code> (adapter)</td><td>2</td><td><code>lcd</code> index = (A &lt;&lt; 2) | common</td><td>MECHANISM (4 commons)</td></tr><tr><td><code>FLASH_LANE_A</code> (adapter)</td><td>2</td><td>A of the flash lane; bit 0 L, bit 1 X</td><td>TABLE-READ (site naming)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s14\">14. Self-check vectors</h4>\n<p>All of these use synthetic programs only. \"Program at 3:7\" means the bytes are placed at page (3, 7) on consecutive P_L steps 00, 20, 30, 38, 3C, 3E, 1F, ... (§3). The trace values are taken after each ABI <code>step()</code>, following a <code>reset()</code>, on SM511 with no melody memory unless one is stated. <code>div</code> is the divider count. With no melody memory (<code>null</code>) the engine does nothing and <code>rOut</code> stays 0 (§11.4 step 1). This happens only in these synthetic vectors: the site always passes the 4,096-byte melody memory for SM511/SM512 (<code>60-ABI.md</code> §1).</p>\n<p><b>A. Clock-select timeline.</b> Program at 3:7: <code>60 36</code>, <code>60 37</code>, <code>T</code> to itself (<code>BC</code>, i.e. P_L 0x3C, at step index 4).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>ran</th><th>pc after</th><th>div</th><th>clkDiv after</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(reset)</td><td>none</td><td>0xDC0</td><td>0</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>CLKHI (slot charged 4)</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>4</td><td><b>2</b></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>CLKLO (slot charged 2)</td><td>0xDFC</td><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>T</td><td>0xDFC</td><td>10</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>T</td><td>0xDFC</td><td>14</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A <code>reset()</code> after a CLKHI returns <code>clkDiv</code> to 4. With only CLKHI followed by a self-loop, γ first becomes 1 after slot 16,383 (4 + 2 × 16,382 = 32,768 ticks).</p>\n<p><b>B. ADD11 with carry, and a skip over a 2-byte instruction.</b> Program: <code>27</code> (LAX 7), <code>10</code> (EXC 0), <code>29</code> (LAX 9), <code>67</code> (SC), <code>09</code> (ADD11), <code>5F 12</code> (LBL), <code>23</code> (LAX 3), T self.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>ran</th><th>pc after</th><th>Acc</th><th>C</th><th>B_M:B_L</th><th>div</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>LAX</td><td>0xDE0</td><td>7</td><td>0</td><td>0:0</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>EXC (M ← 7)</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0:0</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>LAX</td><td>0xDF8</td><td>9</td><td>0</td><td>0:0</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>SC</td><td>0xDFC</td><td>9</td><td>1</td><td>0:0</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>ADD11: 9+7+1 = 17</td><td>0xDFE</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0:0</td><td>20 (skip pending)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>SKIP (both bytes of LBL)</td><td>0xDEF</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td><b>0:0</b> (LBL not run)</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>LAX</td><td>0xDF7</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>0:0</td><td>28</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>C. LAX succession.</b> <code>21 22 23</code> → Acc = 1 after all three (the first loads).</p>\n<p><b>D. LB fold and SBM.</b> <code>45</code>, <code>40</code>, <code>4C</code>, <code>48</code>, <code>02</code> (SBM), <code>43</code> → B_M:B_L after each = 1:13, 0:0, 0:15, 0:14, (SBM) 0:14, 3:0. SBM never shows in B_M.</p>\n<p><b>E. DTA.</b> If the count is 0x5FFC before a DTA slot, the slot's 4 ticks bring it to 0x6000 = 24,576, and Acc = 12.</p>\n<p><b>F. 1S wake timeline (slow clock).</b> Program: 3:7 <code>5D</code> (CEND); 1:0 <code>58</code> (TIS), <code>5D</code>. Step 1 runs CEND: div 4, halt true. Each later step adds 4 ticks and returns null. <b>Step 8,192</b> completes the wrap: 4 + 4 × 8,191 = 32,768, so div becomes 0 and γ = 1. The machine wakes to 0x400, and the same step runs TIS: div 4, no skip because γ was 1, γ ← 0, pc 0x420. Step 8,193 runs CEND at 0x420.</p>\n<p><b>G. K wake.</b> Program: 3:7 <code>5D</code>; 1:0 T self. Step 1: CEND (pc 0xDE0, div 4, halt true). Step 2: halted, div 8. Then set <code>inFixedRow = 0</code>, <code>inRows = [2]</code>, and call <code>updateKLine()</code>: the wake line rises and <b>the part wakes inside that call</b> (§7): <code>halt</code> is false and <code>pc</code> is 0x400 at once, div still 8. Step 3 runs T at the vector: pc 0x400, div 12. Step 4: T again, div 16. [MEASURED 1 Nov 2026 on the reference through the ABI. <em>Corrected 1 Nov 2026:</em> this vector said <code>halt</code> stayed true until step 3, which described the old <code>wakeRelatch: \"external\"</code> rule, not the default.]</p>\n<p><b>H. KTA after PTW.</b> Set <code>inRows = [5,0,0,0,0,0,0,0]</code> (no <code>updateKLine</code>). Program: <code>63</code> (WS), <code>6D</code> (PTW), <code>50</code> (KTA). After WS: W′ = 1, W = 0. After PTW: W = 1 and K pending = 5. After KTA: Acc = 5.</p>\n<p><b>I. WS/WR/PTW.</b> <code>63 62 63</code> → W′ = 0b101 = 5, W = 0. Then <code>6D</code> → W = 5.</p>\n<p><b>J. Melody word decode.</b> 0x25 = 10 0101 → code 5 (la), bit 4 = 0 (lower octave: half periods 18, 18, 18, 20; 885.62 Hz), bit 5 = 1 (32 periods = 4,096 ticks = 125 ms). 0x12 → code 2 (do), upper octave, 16 periods. 0x01 → stop. 0x0E → silent, no flag.</p>\n<p><b>K. Tone half-period worked example.</b> Melody memory filled with 0x12 (do, upper). Program: <code>60 31</code> (SME), T self. During slot 1's ticks 1–4, ME = 0, but the duty counter still advances (phase 0, length 7). Then the pin edges, numbered by tick since ACL, are: <b>8↑ 16↓ 24↑ 32↓ 39↑ 47↓ ...</b>. The phases are 7, 8, 8, 8 ticks, so 31 ticks make two cycles (2114.06 Hz). With 0x02 (lower octave) the edges are <b>15↑ 31↓ 47↑ 63↓ 77↑</b>: phases of 14, 16, 16, 16, i.e. 62 ticks for two cycles.</p>\n<p><b>L. Stop flag and TMEL.</b> Melody word 0 = 0x01 (stop). Program: <code>60 32</code> (TMEL), <code>27</code>, <code>60 32</code>, <code>25</code>, T self. Step 1: the ticks raise MES, TMEL skips, MES ← 0. Step 2: SKIP (the LAX 7), and MES = 1 again. Step 3: TMEL skips, MES 0. Step 4: SKIP, MES 1. Step 5: T. Acc stays 0.</p>\n<p><b>M. Melody step timing.</b> Melody word 0 = 0x20 (rest, long), word 1 = 0x00. With a T self-loop, the address goes 0→1 after slot 1,024 (div 4,096) and 1→2 after slot 1,536 (div 6,144).</p>\n<p><b>N. Jumps.</b> LAX 11 then ATPL at P_L 0x20 → pc 0xDEB. TML <code>69 8A</code> at 3:7:00 → pc 0x84A, S = (3, 7, 0x30), R = (0, 0, 0). TM <code>C5</code> with cover-page byte 0x95 at image offset 0x005 → P_U 2, P_M 4, P_L 0x15 (pc 0x915), S = (3, 7, 0x20). <code>BF</code> (T to 0x3F) → the next step returns <code>stopped: \"p_l-locked\"</code>, with the slot's ticks still charged.</p>\n<p><b>O. Arithmetic edge cases.</b> LAX 11, SC, ROT → Acc 13, C 1. LAX 9, DC → Acc 3, no skip, C unchanged. LAX 9, ADX 11 → Acc 4, skip, C unchanged. An undefined prefix <code>60 99</code> consumes 2 bytes and one slot.</p>\n<p><b>P. Plane (SM512 vs SM511).</b> Program: <code>5F 50</code> <code>2F</code> <code>10</code> (Q0 ← 15), <code>5F 60</code> <code>23</code> <code>10</code> (R0 ← 3), <code>5F 70</code> <code>21</code> <code>10</code> (S0 ← 1), <code>23</code> <code>59</code> (L ← 3), <code>22</code> <code>5C</code> (X ← 2), then <code>updateLcd()</code>. SM512 <code>lcd</code> = <code>0001 0001 0000 0000 | 0001 0000 0000 0000 | 0001 0003 0000 0000 | 0001 0001 0001 0001</code> (A0 R, A1 S, A2 flash, A3 Q). SM511 gives the same first 12 entries and has no A3.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-30-sm511-sm512-s15\">15. Where this chapter's facts came from</h4>\n<p><code>src/sm511.mjs</code> (decoders :73–:131; vectors and clock :182–:206; <code>CHOICES_511</code> :211–:474; handlers :709–:820; <code>selectClock</code> :824; <code>powerOn</code> :838; <code>wakeRequested</code> :884; <code>tickOscillator</code> :908; <code>stepInstruction</code> :957). <code>src/sm512.mjs</code> (:116 <code>SM512_PROFILE</code>; <code>CHOICES_512</code> :138, :163). <code>src/melody511.mjs</code> (:91–:98 constants; <code>tick</code> :159; <code>preset</code> :152). <code>src/melody.mjs</code> (:333 table; :370 half periods). <code>src/cpu.mjs</code> (skip retire :190; shared rows :300–:560). <code>src/pc.mjs</code> (:185 rule; :274 stack). <code>src/timing.mjs</code> (:88 tick; :118 readF). <code>src/lcd.mjs</code> <code>plane</code>. <code>spec/isa_sm511.mjs</code> (p170 table, <code>PREFIX_60</code> :153, <code>UNLISTED</code> :196). <code>adapter/site-abi.mjs</code> (<code>_init511</code> :763; <code>_toLcdArray511</code> :784; <code>_surface511</code> :806, <code>step</code> :830, <code>clkDiv</code> :849, <code>rOut</code> :875). <code>reference/READ-sm511-sm512.md</code> §4–§8. Instruments: <code>tools/sm511_lockstep.mjs</code>, <code>tools/melody511_check.mjs</code>. Board §26.3, §28, §29.2, §29.5. The vectors in §14 were produced by running the ABI on synthetic program images (scratch scripts, not committed).</p></section>";
function gnwSm511EmulationTabHTML(d, part) {
  return '<div class="gbemu gwemu">' + GNW_EMU_SM511_HTML +
    (typeof GNW_EMU_SHARED_HTML === "string" ? (typeof gnwEmuSharedFor === "function" ? gnwEmuSharedFor("gwemu-30-sm511-sm512") : GNW_EMU_SHARED_HTML) : "") + '</div>';
}
